{"answer":"Factor x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2). Thus, for x≠2, (x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2. The original expression has domain R\\{2}. Although x+2 is defined at every real number, it is equivalent to the original expression only on R\\{2}; at x=2 the original is undefined while x+2=4.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator as a difference of squares, then cancel the common nonzero factor on the original domain.","id":"factor-and-cancel","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly establishes the equality for every permitted input and retains the excluded input from the original denominator."},{"description":"Treat x+2 as an everywhere-equivalent replacement without retaining the denominator restriction.","id":"unrestricted-cancellation","outcome":"rejected","reason":"At x=2 the original quotient is undefined, whereas x+2 is defined and equals 4."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the difference-of-squares identity in factor-numerator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"factorization","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x, x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2).","step_ids":["factor-numerator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the denominator condition and factorization.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["factorization"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x with x≠2, (x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2.","step_ids":["identify-domain","cancel-on-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the original denominator x-2.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of the original expression is R\\{2}.","step_ids":["identify-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Compared the original expression and its simplification at the excluded input.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["simplification","original-domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"non-equivalence-everywhere","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The simplified expression x+2 is not equivalent to the original expression at every real number; their equality holds exactly on the original domain R\\{2}.","step_ids":["compare-excluded-input"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-numerator","justification":"Expanding (x-2)(x+2) gives x^2-4.","statement":"Factor the numerator: x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"identify-domain","justification":"A real quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero; x-2≠0 exactly when x≠2.","statement":"The original quotient is defined exactly for real x≠2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-numerator","identify-domain"],"id":"cancel-on-domain","justification":"For x≠2, the factor x-2 is nonzero, so ((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2.","statement":"On the original domain, cancel x-2 to obtain x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cancel-on-domain","identify-domain"],"id":"compare-excluded-input","justification":"At x=2, the original denominator is zero, so the quotient is undefined; the polynomial x+2 is defined and has value 4.","statement":"The expressions differ in definedness at x=2."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
